## Introduction
Atomically precise metal nanoclusters (NCs) as a prototype with unique size-dependent optical and electronic properties and welldefined composition and structure have attracted extensive attention, especially in catalysis. In comparison to conventional metal NCs/nanoparticles, atomically precise metal NCs not only can be efficient catalysts with high catalytic activity, but also act as model catalysts to reveal structure-activity relationship[1-3]. The ultrasmall size of NCs endows high surface-to volume ratio and abundant surface atoms, contributing to its high catalytic activity. However, the ultrasmall size is a double-edged sword which induces the high surface free energy and poor stability of NCs, leading to the aggregation and performance loss during the catalytic process [4-6]. Therefore, how to pursue high activity as well as keep good stability at the same time is still a puzzle in NCsbased catalysis.
Introducing porous matrix to support NCs has been considered as a potential method to improve its stability and catalytic activity. Compared with traditional porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with ultrahigh surface area, highly ordered structure, and tunable chemical functionalities hold great promise to immobilize and uniformly separate NCs [7-9] One of the common methods to encapsulate NCs into MOFs is in situ chemical reduction metal precursor in the pre-synthesized MOFs [10,1l]. By this method, the NCs are usually located on the outer surface of the MOF and easy to fall off, which hinders recycling use [12]. Besides, this method can hardly guarantee the atomic accuracy of NCs, because elaborate thermodynamic selection, kinetic-control reduction, and size-focusing growth or separation procedure are generally required to synthesize precise NCs [7]. The other mainstream method is epitaxial growth MOFs on the pre-synthesized NCs via coordination interaction or electrostatic attraction [8,12]. Due to the poor thermal stability of NCs, this method only can be applicable to the MOFs that can be synthesized at low temperature, resulting in a huge limitation in the type of composites. Therefore, it is essential to develop an effective strategy to integrate NCs and MOFs.
Owing to the diversity of functional groups in NCs and MOFs, it provides an opportunity to build a bridge to combine NCs and MOFs based on the covalent bonding. This bonding form can not only strongly stabilize NCs, avoid aggregation and detachment in catalysis, but also initiate strong metal-support interaction to create synergies and boost the catalytic activity. In this work, we have proposed the “covalence bridge” strategy to improve the stability and activity.Previously, Jiang et al. utilized Au-carbene covalent bonds to in situ synthesize Au NC/UiO-68-NHC $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathrm{NHC}=\mathrm{N}.}\end{array}$ -heterocyclic carbene). However, the cluster obtained by in situ preparation hardly possesses precise structure [5]. Therefore, we pre-synthesized atomically precise $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ NCs (L-Cys is short for L-Cysteine) and bonded with UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ via amide covalence link. Due to the strong linkage, the UiO
$66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH_{2}}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Au_{25}}(\mathrm{L}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Cys})_{18}$ exhibited excellent stability after long-term photocatalytic hydrogen evolution compared with its counterpart made by physically mix ( $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ (PET is short for 2-phenylethanethiol). In addition, the “covalence bridge played a role of charge transport channel, which facilitates photogenerated $\mathrm{e{-}h^{\ast}}$ separation and suppresses the recombination of them. Therefore, benefiting from the strong metal-support interaction, the UiO-6 $6{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Cys})_{18}$ showed extraordinary $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rates $(17012.045~\upmu\mathrm{molcdotg^{-1}\cdot h^{-1}})$ ，which is 2l times higher than that of UiO-66 ${\cdot\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}}$
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18}}}$
UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ were synthesized according to previous reports[13,14].  UiO $66\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18}}}$ was prepared by dehydration condensation reaction. $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ solution (l mg, 1 mL $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ ）was added into the UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ solution $(10\mathrm{~\small~\mg},10\mathrm{~\small~\mL~}\mathrm{~\small~H_{2}O}).$ ，then $0.5~\mathrm{~g~}$ 1-ethyl-3-(3- dimethylaminopropyl） carbodiimide (EDCl） and $0.177\mathrm{~g~N~}$ hydroxybenzotrizole (HOBT） were added, and the mixture was rapidly stirred at room temperature for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ Finally, the product was collected by centrifugation, washed with ethanol and dried in a vacuum oven at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
## 2.2 Synthesis of UiO- $66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}$
$\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}$ was synthesized according to our previous report [15]. $\mathrm{UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ was synthesized by chemical impregnation method, $\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}$ solution $(1\ \mathrm{~mg},\ 1\ \mathrm{~mL}$ dichloromethane (DCM)） was added into the $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ solution $(10~\mathrm{mg},~10~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{DCM})$ and stirred vigorously at room temperature for $^{\mathrm{~2~h~}}$ 、 Finally, the product was collected by centrifugation and dried in a vacuum oven at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$
## 2.3 Characterization
Ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) absorption spectra were recorded on a Techcomp UV1000 spectrophotometer. Morphology of sample was observed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM2100). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS） spectra were performed by an ESCALAB 250Xi XPS system. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were obtained with Cu Kα radiation on SmartLab 9KW. Loading amount was determined by inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS, iCAP
7400 Duo). Electron spin resonance (ESR) spectra were obtained from JEOL JES-FA200 ESR spectrometer. Fluorescence spectra at diferent temperatures were obtained from Hitachi F-7000 fluorescence spectrophotometer with low temperature accessory. The femtosecond pump-probe transient absorption (TA) measurements were performed using a regenerative amplified Ti:sapphire laser system (coherent; ${800}\mathrm{nm}$ ,70 fs, 6 mJ-pulse-, and $1\ \mathrm{kHz}$ repetition rate) as the laser source and a femto-TA100 spectrometer (Time-Tech Spectra).
## 2.4 Photoelectrochemical measurements
Photocurrent, Mott-Schottky, and Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests were tested on CHI760D (Shanghai, Chen hua Co., Ltd., China) electrochemical workstation. $2~\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst was dispersed in $2~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol containing $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ nafion, and the mixed solution $(100\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was uniformly coated on indiumtin oxide (ITO) glass as working electrode for photocurrent test, or $(10~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ dropped on the glassy carbon electrode for Mott-Schottky and EIS tests. Using the three-electrode test method, the counter electrode was Pt plate; the reference electrode was $\mathrm{Ag/AgCl};$ the electrolyte was 0.1 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ The light source was 300 W Xenon lamp with a cut-off filter $\left(>\ 420\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ . The photocurrent was measured at bias potential of $+0.5\mathrm{V}$ and under chopped light with $10s$ intervals. The test frequency of Mott-Schottky was 500, 1,000, and ${1,500}\mathrm{Hz},$ and the test of EIS was under dark conditions, and the voltage was $-1.5\mathrm{V}$
## 2.5 Photocatalytic hydrogen production
$5\ \mathrm{mg}$ catalyst，triethanolamine（TEOA） $(100\ \mathrm{mL},\ 10\ \mathrm{wt.}\%)$ as sacrificial reagent, and $0.02\mathrm{~g~Er~B~}$ as photosensitizer were mixed uniformly, and the suspension was stirred and purged with nitrogen for $\sim20\mathrm{min}$ to remove air. The light source was a $300\mathrm{W}$ Xenon lamp with a $>420\ \mathrm{nm}$ filter. For hydrogen generation, $200{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of the headspace was injected into the gas chromatography (S Kexiao GC-l690, using high-purity Ar as the carrier gas) using a thermal conductivity detector (TCD） and was quantified by standard curve.
## 3 Results and discussion
The formation of amide covalent bond in UiO-66 ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}$ $\mathrm{Cys})_{18}$ was further verified by Fourier transforms infrared (FT-IR) and XPS. For UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ it showed several characteristic peaks, which were located at 3,350 and $3{,}465~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ascribed to the symmetric and asymmetric $_\mathrm{N-H}$ stretching modes [l6] at 1,255 and $1{,}332\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to the $\mathrm{C-N}$ stretching [17],and at
$1{,}050\ c m^{-1}$ attributed to the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ rocking vibrations, respectively [18].For $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18},}}$ a new peak at $1{,}678\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ emerged, indicating the formation of amide $(-\mathrm{NH-C=O})$ (Fig. 2(a)) [19]. In addition, the $\mathrm{~N~}$ 1s XPS spectrum of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ displayed a new peak at $401.5\ \mathrm{eV};$ also confirming the formation of amide bond (Fig.2(b)) [19]. However, UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ did not exhibit the typical amide feature peak in FT-IR and XPS spectra (Fig.S3 in the ESM). Furthermore, an obvious charge transfer took place between $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ NCs and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ .For $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18},}}$ the binding energy of $\mathrm{Zr}$ 3d showed $\sim0.30\ \mathrm{eV}$ decrease compared with UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ indicating the increase in electron density of $\mathrm{Zr}$ In comparison to the $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ NCs, Au 4f and $\mathrm{~\normalfont~S~2p~}$ peaks of UiO- $66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Cys})_{18}$ showed about 0.32 and 0.3l eV positively shift, suggesting the reduction in electron density of Au and S (Figs. 2(c)-2(e)). The XPS peak shifts are attributed to the interface electron transfer between $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ and UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ confirming the strong metal-support synergistic interaction due to the existence of covalence bridge, which is beneficial for the transfer of photogenerated electron-hole pairs, so as to improve the photocatalytic activity [20]. Nevertheless, all of the chemical environment of $\Zint\Gamma,$ Au, and S in UiO- $66{-}\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ were almost unchanged after ${\mathrm{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}{\mathrm{NCs}}$ combining with UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ which is because that there is only simple physical contact between them (Fig.S4 in the ESM).
From the Mott-Schottky plots, the conduction band (CB） of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}.$ ，Ui $\mathrm{O-66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18},}}$ andUiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ were estimated to be $-0.73$ 。 $-1.81$ and $-0.90\mathrm{V}$ (vs. NHE, $\mathrm{pH}=6.8\$ ),respectively, which are all lower than the potential  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ reduction( $_{-0.40\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs.NHE, $\mathrm{pH}~=~6.8\rangle$ _， indicating that they are suitable for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production (Fig.S5 in the ESM)[l4,21]. Therefore, the photocatalytic performance of asprepared samples was evaluated by $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production from water splitting. Under visible light irradiation, pristine $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibited negligible $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate $(\sim\ 188.425\ \upmu\mathrm{mol{\cdotg^{-1}\cdot h^{-1}}})$ After covalencebindingwith $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ NCs,the photocatalytic activity was remarkably improved. The $0.25\ \mathrm{wt.\%}$ $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ NCs loading on the $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ showed the maximum $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate of $\sim17,012.045\mathrm{~\\upmumol{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}}$ 。90 times higher than bare ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2},$ and 2l times as high as physical combination of similar content $\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}$ NCs and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Fig. 3(a), and Figs. S6-S8 in the ESM). Besides, UiO$66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH_{2}}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Au_{25}}(\mathrm{L}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Cys})_{18}$ performed higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation rate than other previous reported catalysts (Table Sl in the ESM). The results indicate that Au NCs can effectively enhance the photocatalytic activity, and the covalent linkage is much more favor for charge transfer, suppressing e-h recombination and thus for better photocatalytic performance. Moreover, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ retained well-satisfied $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate in the consecutive four runs, while the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ was greatly decreased by $70\%$ only after one cycle (Fig.3(b)). The loss in catalytic activity of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ is mainly due to the aggregation and shedding of clusters caused by weak physical binding during photocatalysis (Fig. S9 and Table $S2$ in the ESM). After photocatalytic reaction, $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18}}}$ maintainedthe structure, morphology, and chemical environment as before (Figs. S9 and S10 in the ESM).However, the chemical state of Au and $\mathrm{Zr}$ in $\mathrm{UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ exhibited significant change. The Au 4f XPS peaks showed $\sim0.26\ \mathrm{eV}$ positively shift, while Zr 3d XPS peaks showed \~ $0.28\ \mathrm{\eV}$ negatively shift, compared with the original UiO- ${56{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}/{\mathrm{Au}_{25}}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}$ (Fig.S10 in the ESM). These results reveal that $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18}}}$ exhibits enhanced photostability due to the covalence bridge.
To explore the mechanism of the enhanced photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production activity of UiO-66 ${\sf5-N H}_{2}{\mathrm{-Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18},$ we investigated it from the aspects of thermodynamics, the generation, separation, and transfer efficiency of $^{\mathrm{e-h}}$ pairs. It is well known that introducing cocatalyst can reduce $E_{\mathrm{{R}}}$ (the overpotential for water reduction) and thus improve the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate on the basis of thermodynamic principle. The on-set overpotential of hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is the reflection of the ability of a cocatalyst to lower $E_{\mathrm{{R}}}$ [14,22]. As can be seen from the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves, the UiC $)-66\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18}}}$ exhibited lowest on-set overpotential, indicating that UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ was thermodynamically superior to UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Fig.3(c)). Besides, after combining with Au NCs, the absorption spectrum of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ basically remained the original profile and showed the characteristic peak of Au NCs, and the absorption edge of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ displayed a significant red shift, and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{-}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}.$ $\mathrm{Cys})_{18}$ had the maximum light harvesting (Fig. S1l(a) in the ESM). The corresponding Tauc plot reveals that the Eg of UiO $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ is 2.47 eV, which is lower than UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ $(2.56~\mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(2.89\ \mathrm{\eV})$ (Figs. S1l(b)-S1l(d) in the ESM). In addition, UiC $\partial{\mathrm{-}}66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}-$ $\mathrm{Cys})_{18}$ possessed the strongest photocurrent response followed by UiO-6 ${56}\mathrm{-NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ indicating that UiO$66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH_{2}}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Au_{25}}(\mathrm{L}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Cys})_{18}$ can promote the generation of more photoexcited charges (Fig.3(d)). The electron transfer kinetics also need to be taken into account. The smallest interfacial charge transfer resistance of $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18}}}$ confirmed its best electron transport capability (Fig.3(e)). The steady-state photoluminescence (PL） intensity followed the order: UiO-66- $\mathrm{{NH_{2}}>\Delta U i O-66-N H_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}>U i O-66-N H_{2}\mathrm{{-Au_{25}(L-C y s)_{18}},}}$ also demonstrating the rapidest charge separation in UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\mathrm{-Cys}})_{18}$ (Fig. 3(f)). From both thermodynamics and kinetics aspects, all of these results demonstrate that benefiting from the covalence bridge, $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18}}}$ exhibited stronger ability of carriers generation, more effective e-h separation and higher charge immigration efficiency.
ESR, ultrafast TA, and temperature-dependent PL spectroscopy were further used to insight into the mechanism. Under visible light irradiation, all samples showed a new ESR signal at $g=$ 2.0023, corresponding to the generation of oxygen-centered active sites in $\mathrm{Zr}$ -oxo clusters through link-to-cluster electron transfer (LCCT). [14,23] The ESR signals of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ were similar and relatively weak, while UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}{-}\mathrm{Cys})_{18}$ exhibited a stronger signal, which is attributed to the acceleration of photoinduced electron transfer (Fig.4(a)). Ultrafast TA spectroscopy was conducted to track in real time the charge transfer dynamics. For all samples, the TA spectra profiles were similar with no essential variations under different probe delays. When probed at the same delay times, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{Au}}_{25}(\mathrm{{L}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{Cys}})_{18}$ exhibited a weaker stimulated emission (a negative signal in the range of 450 and $550~\mathrm{nm}$ ）compared with UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ and ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2},$ suggesting the efficient quenching excited state by covalent linked $\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-Cys})_{18}\mathrm{NCs}$ (Fig. $4(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{b}})$ and Fig.S12 in the ESM). This is in the accordance with the PL emission spectra, which also demonstrates that covalent linked Au NCs have the stronger ability to quench the PL of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ than physically connected Au NCs, as shown in Fig.3(f). From the TA kinetics traces of excited state absorption (ESA） at
$650~\mathrm{~nm}$ 。 $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18}}}$ exhibited the shortest average relaxation lifetime of $^{56.6}\mathrm{~ps},$ indicating that the fastest charge transfer between $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and Au NCs, again consistent with its highest photocatalytic activity (Fig. 4(c)). UiO$66{-}\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ showed higher activity than pure UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ however the excited state charge carrier dynamics of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18}}$ was not superior to pure MOF. Therefore, its high photocatalytic activity is mainly attributed to its strong light absorption and low overpotential for water reduction (Fig.3(c) and Fig. S1l in the ESM).
In addition, the activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ was used to evaluate the difficulty of a reaction combining both thermodynamic and dynamic factors. With the help of temperature-dependent PL and Arrhenius equation, the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of UiC $){\displaystyle-66-\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Cys})_{18}},$ UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH_{2}/A u_{25}(P E T)_{18},}$ and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were estimated as 34.95, 35.60,and $43.76~\mathrm{meV}$ 。respectively (Fig.4(d) and Figs. S13 and S14 in the ESM). The result demonstrates that covalent linked Au NCs can effectively reduce the reaction energy barrier and improve the activity.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, a “covalence bridge” strategy was developed to effectively connect atomically precise NCs and MOF. Take UiO$66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Cys})_{18}$ as the example, with the help of the amide covalent linkage, it not only provides the assurance for the long term photostability, but also offers strong metal-support interaction for enhanced photocatalytic activity. A series of photoelectrochemical and  spectroscopic  experiments  were conducted to reveal the underlying reason of the performance differencebetweenU $\mathrm{JiO-66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18}}}$ and its counterparts from both thermodynamic and kinetic aspects. Due to the formation of covalence bridge, UiO $\mathrm{\cdot66-NH_{2}\mathrm{-Au_{25}(L\mathrm{-}C y s)_{18}}}$ can effectively lower the overpotential for water reduction, reduce the reaction energy barrier, promote charge separation, suppress e-h recombination, and thus higher photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production activity. This work not only provides inspirations for developing NCs-MOFs with high activity and stability, but also offers in depth understanding on the effect of link way between NCs and MOFs on thermodynamic and charge transfer kinetics process.
